From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 18:24:12 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: qcom_fg: fix inverted
 POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_CURRENT_NOW sign

qcom_fg_get_current() and qcom_fg_gen3_get_current() report the raw,
sign-extended BATT_CURRENT SRAM value directly. On taimen (PMI8998,
gen3 fuel gauge) that raw value is positive while the battery is
discharging and negative while it is charging -- the opposite of the
kernel's power-supply convention, where CURRENT_NOW is positive for
charging and negative for discharging (see e.g. the documented
convention in 88pm860x_battery.c).

This was measured directly on taimen hardware while fixing the
sign-extension helper in a prior commit: -8789 uA idle at 99% charge
(charging) and +250000 uA sustained under an 8-way CPU burn
(discharging). Negate the scaled value so the sign matches what
userspace expects from every other power-supply driver.

Both qcom_fg_get_current() (pre-gen3, PMI8996 and older) and
qcom_fg_gen3_get_current() (PMI8998 and newer) read the same
BATT_CURRENT register family and share the same sign convention, so
fix both for consistency even though only the gen3 path is exercised
on taimen.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/power/supply/qcom_fg.c | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_fg.c b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_fg.c
index a79fe65cf4b1..5fa1f0b0f7d9 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/qcom_fg.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/qcom_fg.c
@@ -374,7 +374,7 @@ static int qcom_fg_get_current(struct qcom_fg_chip *chip, int *val)
 	}
 	//handle rev 1 too
 	temp = sign_extend32(readval[1] << 8 | readval[0], 15);
-	*val = div_s64((s64)temp * 152587, 1000);
+	*val = -div_s64((s64)temp * 152587, 1000);
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -429,7 +429,7 @@ static int qcom_fg_gen3_get_current(struct qcom_fg_chip *chip, int *val)
 	}
 	//handle rev 1 too
 	temp = sign_extend32(readval[1] << 8 | readval[0], 15);
-	*val = div_s64((s64)temp * 488281, 1000);
+	*val = -div_s64((s64)temp * 488281, 1000);
 	return 0;
 }
 
